<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs">

  <?include ComponentGuids.wxi ?>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Remembering WHERE the previous version was installed.

       The service itself never needs this: at runtime the install folder is AppContext.BaseDirectory (the
       exe's own folder) and the data folder is Jenkins:Agent:DataDirectory read from the appsettings.json
       sitting beside it. That chain only works once you already hold the exe.

       An upgrade is upstream of it. Windows Installer has to decide where to PUT the exe before anything can
       be read back, and the package recorded nothing to decide with - so INSTALLFOLDER and DATAFOLDER both
       reset to their defaults on every upgrade. Two consequences, neither cosmetic:

         * A non-default install folder was silently relocated to Program Files. appsettings.json is written
           by a custom action and is NOT a tracked MSI file, so it stays behind at the old path - the new
           install has no config, UpgradeConfig has nothing to reconcile, and the service comes up with no
           URL and no secret.
         * A non-default DATAFOLDER meant the DataFolderAcl component created and ACL'd a stray
           %ProgramData%\JenkinsAsService, granting the service account write on a folder nothing uses. The
           real data folder kept working (the config still pointed at it), so nothing failed loudly; and
           uninstall's purge reads DataDirectory out of the config, so the stray outlived the product.

       Both locations are therefore recorded at install time and searched for at the start of the next one.
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <Fragment>

    <!-- ─── 1. Record. Written on every install, including the new product of a major upgrade, so the values
             track the package rather than going stale. Tracked as a normal component, so a genuine uninstall
             removes the key and a reinstall does not inherit a dead path.

             HKLM, never HKCU: the product is perMachine and its custom actions run as SYSTEM, so a per-user
             hive would be both wrong and unreadable from the account that needs it.

             Each architecture owns a named subkey - SOFTWARE\JenkinsAsService\x64, ...\x86 and ...\arm64 -
             so WHICH key holds a path is itself the statement of which architecture is installed. That is the
             only way a later package can tell a same-arch upgrade from a cross-arch migration: all three
             share an UpgradeCode, and FindRelatedProducts cannot distinguish them. It needs no separate value
             to go stale, and it is legible to anyone poking at the machine with regedit.

             No Bitness attribute - the component inherits the package's, so the x64 and arm64 packages write
             the native 64-bit view and the x86 package writes WOW6432Node. (On Windows on ARM the native and
             the emulated-x64 registry views are the same one; only 32-bit x86 is redirected.) The searches
             below therefore read each key in the matching view, and read all three, which is what makes the
             cross-architecture case detectable. ─── -->
    <!-- A GUID PER ARCHITECTURE, unlike ServiceComponent, and the difference matters. Component GUIDs are
         what Windows Installer reference-counts, and a GUID must always name the same resource - but these
         three components write different keys, two of them in a different registry view. Sharing one GUID
         would make a forced cross-arch migration install the new component (refcount 2) and then remove the
         old product's reference (back to 1), so the OLD architecture's key would never be deleted. Two keys
         would then exist, and the next package would see two architectures installed at once and block
         against a product that is no longer there. ServiceComponent can share a GUID precisely because every
         package installs the same file to the same path. -->
    <?if $(var.InstallerPlatform) = "x64" ?>
      <?define LocationComponentGuid = "F06D514A-43FA-4FAE-B1F8-4B7A050B19E3" ?>
    <?elseif $(var.InstallerPlatform) = "arm64" ?>
      <?define LocationComponentGuid = "3284A7DD-E5CA-4553-8C45-74F06E4AE8BE" ?>
    <?else?>
      <?define LocationComponentGuid = "F49566AD-A619-40DF-BE18-CA39E6ABA61D" ?>
    <?endif?>
    <Component Id="InstallLocationRegistry" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER"
               Guid="$(var.LocationComponentGuid)">
      <RegistryKey Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\JenkinsAsService\$(var.InstallerPlatform)">
        <RegistryValue Name="InstallPath" Type="string" Value="[INSTALLFOLDER]" KeyPath="yes" />
        <RegistryValue Name="DataPath" Type="string" Value="[DATAFOLDER]" />
      </RegistryKey>
    </Component>

    <!-- ─── 2. Search. Four sources per location, deliberately overlapping.

             The registry values are the primary source and are read in BOTH views and under ALL THREE
             architecture names, so any package sees an install made by any other one.

             The two 64-bit names are read with Bitness="always64" and the x86 name with always32, matching
             where each package's own component wrote. arm64 is NOT a third view: Windows on ARM gives native
             ARM64 and emulated x64 processes the same 64-bit registry, so the arm64 and x64 keys are
             siblings under one hive and only x86 is redirected into WOW6432Node.

             ComponentSearch is the fallback: it resolves the directory of a component by GUID out of
             Windows Installer's own component registration, which is architecture-neutral and needs nothing
             this package wrote. It covers the case the registry cannot - a product installed by a version
             that predates this key, which has no InstallPath to find. Those installs also carry no recorded
             Platform, so a cross-architecture migration away from one cannot be detected; see the gate below.
             ─── -->
    <Property Id="JAS_INSTALLDIR_X64" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="SearchInstallPathX64" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\JenkinsAsService\x64"
                      Name="InstallPath" Type="raw" Bitness="always64" />
    </Property>
    <Property Id="JAS_INSTALLDIR_ARM64" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="SearchInstallPathArm64" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\JenkinsAsService\arm64"
                      Name="InstallPath" Type="raw" Bitness="always64" />
    </Property>
    <Property Id="JAS_INSTALLDIR_X86" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="SearchInstallPathX86" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\JenkinsAsService\x86"
                      Name="InstallPath" Type="raw" Bitness="always32" />
    </Property>
    <Property Id="JAS_INSTALLDIR_COMPONENT" Secure="yes">
      <ComponentSearch Id="SearchInstallByComponent"
                       Guid="$(var.JasServiceComponentGuid)" Type="directory" />
    </Property>

    <Property Id="JAS_DATADIR_X64" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="SearchDataPathX64" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\JenkinsAsService\x64"
                      Name="DataPath" Type="raw" Bitness="always64" />
    </Property>
    <Property Id="JAS_DATADIR_ARM64" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="SearchDataPathArm64" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\JenkinsAsService\arm64"
                      Name="DataPath" Type="raw" Bitness="always64" />
    </Property>
    <Property Id="JAS_DATADIR_X86" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="SearchDataPathX86" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\JenkinsAsService\x86"
                      Name="DataPath" Type="raw" Bitness="always32" />
    </Property>
    <Property Id="JAS_DATADIR_COMPONENT" Secure="yes">
      <ComponentSearch Id="SearchDataByComponent"
                       Guid="$(var.JasDataFolderComponentGuid)" Type="directory" />
    </Property>

    <!-- ─── 3. Apply. SetProperty (a type-51 property-setting custom action) rather than SetDirectory, so the
             scheduling and the precedence are visible in one place instead of being implied.

             Sequence="both" is load-bearing, not shorthand: these have to run in the UI sequence (which
             resolves the paths the wizard displays) AND the execute sequence (which resolves where the install
             actually writes, and is all a silent install runs). Authored once here, WiX emits both rows, so
             the two cannot drift - a condition fixed in only one of two hand-mirrored tables would give a
             wizard install and a /quiet install different install folders, and nothing in CI compares them.
             The explicit Action name is what lets several of these target one property: the generated custom
             action is named after it, so without it the six below would collide on one id.

             Precedence is FIRST match wins, encoded by the `NOT <target>` clause every one of them carries:
             once a higher-priority action has set the property, every later action's condition is false.
             Order: the x64 key, then arm64, then x86, then the component registration. Only one of the three
             keys can exist at a time - the packages share an UpgradeCode and cannot coexist - so the order
             between them settles nothing in practice; it is fixed so the behaviour is stated rather than
             incidental.

             That same clause is what protects an explicit override. Before CostFinalize a directory property
             holds a value ONLY if it was passed on the command line - the Directory table has not been
             costed yet - so `NOT INSTALLFOLDER` reads exactly as "the operator did not pass INSTALLFOLDER=".
             An operator who names a path still gets it; the recovery only fills a vacuum.

             Everything here must run after AppSearch (where the values above are found) and before
             CostFinalize (where directory properties are resolved into real paths). In this package that is
             AppSearch at 50, these immediately after it, CostFinalize at 1000. Both sequences are authored:
             the UI sequence needs the recovered paths for what it displays, the execute sequence for where it
             actually writes. ─── -->
    <!-- The two 64-bit install folders are recovered ONLY by the 64-bit packages, and that is not a tidiness
         choice. A 32-bit package cannot read a 64-bit Program Files path back out of the registry: the search
         itself works, but Windows Installer's WIN64DUALFOLDERS substitution then rewrites the RESULT, mapping
         the `C:\Program Files\` prefix of any folder-shaped AppSearch value to `C:\Program Files (x86)\`.
         Observed in CI, in the verbose log, verbatim:

             WIN64DUALFOLDERS: 'C:\Program Files (x86)\' will substitute 17 characters in
             'C:\Program Files\Jenkins\' folder path.
             PROPERTY CHANGE: Adding JAS_INSTALLDIR_X64 property. Its value is 'C:\Program Files (x86)\Jenkins\'

         So the x86 package would "recover" a path the previous version never used, install beside the real
         one, and leave appsettings.json - with the only copy of the secret - behind at the original location.
         That is the exact failure this whole file exists to prevent, which is why the x86 package refuses the
         migration outright rather than recovering a value it cannot trust (see the gate below).

         The split is 64-bit vs 32-bit, NOT x64 vs everything-else: an arm64 package is a 64-bit package whose
         ProgramFiles6432Folder is the real `C:\Program Files`, so nothing substitutes its recovered paths and
         it reads both 64-bit keys exactly as the x64 package does. Writing the condition against x64 alone
         would have silently stopped ARM64 upgrades recovering their own previous install folder.

         DATAFOLDER is unaffected and is recovered by all three: %ProgramData% is not part of a dual-folder
         pair, so nothing rewrites it. -->
    <?if $(var.InstallerPlatform) = "x86" ?>
      <?define InstallDirChainAnchor = "AppSearch" ?>
    <?else?>
      <SetProperty Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Action="RecoverInstallDirFromX64" Value="[JAS_INSTALLDIR_X64]"
                   After="AppSearch" Sequence="both"
                   Condition="JAS_INSTALLDIR_X64 AND NOT INSTALLFOLDER" />
      <SetProperty Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Action="RecoverInstallDirFromArm64" Value="[JAS_INSTALLDIR_ARM64]"
                   After="RecoverInstallDirFromX64" Sequence="both"
                   Condition="JAS_INSTALLDIR_ARM64 AND NOT INSTALLFOLDER" />
      <?define InstallDirChainAnchor = "RecoverInstallDirFromArm64" ?>
    <?endif?>
    <SetProperty Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Action="RecoverInstallDirFromX86" Value="[JAS_INSTALLDIR_X86]"
                 After="$(var.InstallDirChainAnchor)" Sequence="both"
                 Condition="JAS_INSTALLDIR_X86 AND NOT INSTALLFOLDER" />
    <SetProperty Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Action="RecoverInstallDirFromComponent" Value="[JAS_INSTALLDIR_COMPONENT]"
                 After="RecoverInstallDirFromX86" Sequence="both"
                 Condition="JAS_INSTALLDIR_COMPONENT AND NOT INSTALLFOLDER" />
    <SetProperty Id="DATAFOLDER" Action="RecoverDataDirFromX64" Value="[JAS_DATADIR_X64]"
                 After="RecoverInstallDirFromComponent" Sequence="both"
                 Condition="JAS_DATADIR_X64 AND NOT DATAFOLDER" />
    <SetProperty Id="DATAFOLDER" Action="RecoverDataDirFromArm64" Value="[JAS_DATADIR_ARM64]"
                 After="RecoverDataDirFromX64" Sequence="both"
                 Condition="JAS_DATADIR_ARM64 AND NOT DATAFOLDER" />
    <SetProperty Id="DATAFOLDER" Action="RecoverDataDirFromX86" Value="[JAS_DATADIR_X86]"
                 After="RecoverDataDirFromArm64" Sequence="both"
                 Condition="JAS_DATADIR_X86 AND NOT DATAFOLDER" />
    <SetProperty Id="DATAFOLDER" Action="RecoverDataDirFromComponent" Value="[JAS_DATADIR_COMPONENT]"
                 After="RecoverDataDirFromX86" Sequence="both"
                 Condition="JAS_DATADIR_COMPONENT AND NOT DATAFOLDER" />

    <!-- ─── 4. The cross-architecture gate, which is deliberately ASYMMETRIC.

             All three packages share an UpgradeCode, so MajorUpgrade will happily replace any of them with
             any other and FindRelatedProducts cannot tell the difference. There is no undo: the old product
             is removed at InstallInitialize, before anything can be reconsidered.

             The rule is 64-bit vs 32-bit, not a table of six directed pairs:

               * INTO a 64-bit package (x64 or arm64), from any other architecture: SUPPORTED, opt-in via
                 FORCE_UPGRADE=1. Windows Installer takes PROPERTIES rather than command-line switches, so the
                 opt-in is spelled FORCE_UPGRADE=1 rather than as a dashed flag, and the requirement is
                 identical in the wizard and in a silent install: a silent run has nobody to prompt, and a run
                 that would prompt has no business being easier to get wrong. When it is forced, the recovery
                 above has already pointed INSTALLFOLDER and DATAFOLDER at the existing locations, so the
                 migrated install lands on top of the config and data it inherits. This covers x86 -> x64,
                 x86 -> arm64, and - the one that matters for Windows on ARM - x64 -> arm64, since every
                 ARM64 machine that installed this product before there was an arm64 package is running the
                 x64 build under emulation and should be migrated to the native one.

               * INTO the x86 package, from either 64-bit architecture: REFUSED OUTRIGHT, and FORCE_UPGRADE
                 does not override it. The 32-bit package cannot recover the previous install folder:
                 WIN64DUALFOLDERS rewrites the recovered path to `C:\Program Files (x86)\...` (see the note
                 above the recovery), so the migration would install beside the real one and strand
                 appsettings.json - the only copy of the secret - at the old path. A migration that silently
                 destroys the thing it promises to keep is worse than one that refuses, so the package
                 refuses and says which one to run instead.

             Nothing is lost by the refusal. The bundle never needs that direction: its policy is the
             machine's NATIVE architecture, so it only ever installs x86 on a machine where no 64-bit install
             can exist. Going deliberately from a 64-bit to a 32-bit agent is an uninstall followed by a fresh
             install - which is honest, because the config would have to be re-created at the new location
             either way.

             A type-19 error action rather than a LaunchCondition: LaunchConditions is sequenced at 100, after
             AppSearch (50) but the gate has to sit next to the searches it depends on, and authoring it as an
             error action keeps the two adjacent instead of split across files. It lands immediately after the
             recovery actions above - far ahead of InstallInitialize (1500) and RemoveExistingProducts (1501)
             - so a blocked run aborts before the installed product has been touched at all.
             NOT Installed keeps it off the uninstall and maintenance paths; during a major upgrade the
             incoming ProductCode is not installed yet, so the gate still applies there.

             The condition reads the SEARCH results directly. There is no intermediate "installed platform"
             property, because which key held a path already IS that statement and re-recording it into a
             third property added a value that could only ever agree or be wrong. Note that each 64-bit
             condition below is the exact reading of first-match-wins: a package blocks on ANOTHER
             architecture's key only when its OWN key is absent, so a machine that somehow carries two is
             treated as this package's own architecture rather than blocked against a product that is no
             longer there. An install found only by ComponentSearch states no architecture at all - it
             predates these keys - so a migration away from one is neither detected nor blocked. ─── -->
    <!-- Kept under 255 characters: CustomAction.Target is capped there (ICE03), the same limit that forced
         WriteAdvanced1/2/3 apart. The full explanation lives in docs/installation.html; this has to fit.
         The 64-bit message names its own architecture by expanding InstallerPlatform, so the x64 and arm64
         packages share one string rather than carrying two that say the same thing about different words. -->
    <?if $(var.InstallerPlatform) = "x86" ?>
      <CustomAction Id="BlockArchMigration"
                    Error="[ProductName] is installed as 64-bit. A 32-bit package cannot take over that install - Windows Installer rewrites the recorded path, which would strand your configuration and agent secret. Install the 64-bit package, or the .exe installer." />
      <!-- No FORCE_UPGRADE term: this direction is refused, not gated. Adding an override would only offer a
           way to reach the broken path on purpose. -->
      <?define ArchGateCondition = "(JAS_INSTALLDIR_X64 OR JAS_INSTALLDIR_ARM64) AND NOT Installed" ?>
    <?else?>
      <CustomAction Id="BlockArchMigration"
                    Error="[ProductName] is installed for another architecture; this is the $(var.InstallerPlatform) package. Switching is not reversible. Re-run with FORCE_UPGRADE=1 to migrate (config, secret and data are kept), or use the matching package." />
      <!-- The whole precondition in one condition, rather than a separate action setting a JAS_ARCH_MISMATCH
           flag for this one consumer to read: some other architecture is recorded, this one is not, and the
           migration was not asked for.

           The two 64-bit packages differ only in which three property names fill those three slots, so each
           branch supplies the NAMES and the SHAPE is written once below it. Spelled out per branch instead,
           the policy lived in two ~130-character strings that had to stay parallel by eye - and a fourth
           architecture would have added a third copy. Note that MSI property names are CASE-SENSITIVE, which
           is why the own-arch name is a define rather than JAS_INSTALLDIR_$(var.InstallerPlatform): that
           expands to the lower-case spelling, which is simply a different, always-empty property, so the
           gate would silently stop blocking anything. -->
      <?if $(var.InstallerPlatform) = "x64" ?>
        <?define ArchGateOwn = "JAS_INSTALLDIR_X64" ?>
        <?define ArchGateOthers = "JAS_INSTALLDIR_X86 OR JAS_INSTALLDIR_ARM64" ?>
      <?else?>
        <?define ArchGateOwn = "JAS_INSTALLDIR_ARM64" ?>
        <?define ArchGateOthers = "JAS_INSTALLDIR_X86 OR JAS_INSTALLDIR_X64" ?>
      <?endif?>
      <?define ArchGateCondition = "($(var.ArchGateOthers)) AND NOT $(var.ArchGateOwn) AND NOT FORCE_UPGRADE AND NOT Installed" ?>
    <?endif?>

    <!-- An error action cannot be scheduled with Sequence="both" the way the SetPropertys above are, so this
         is the one thing still authored per sequence: two rows for one action. They carry the same condition
         by CONSTRUCTION rather than by care, both expanding one define - which only became possible once the
         condition stopped comparing against a quoted string literal, since a <?define?> value cannot itself
         carry double quotes. A hand-mirrored divergence would not have been caught: Test-MsiArchMigration.ps1
         only ever runs /quiet, which executes the InstallExecuteSequence row alone. -->
    <InstallUISequence>
      <Custom Action="BlockArchMigration" After="RecoverDataDirFromComponent"
              Condition="$(var.ArchGateCondition)" />
    </InstallUISequence>

    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <Custom Action="BlockArchMigration" After="RecoverDataDirFromComponent"
              Condition="$(var.ArchGateCondition)" />
    </InstallExecuteSequence>

  </Fragment>

</Wix>
